remaining zigzag is an arrow. The horizontal arrows are exactly
\begin{equation}\hypertarget{tag-01TA}{}\label{inv:eq:C-arrows}
\begin{array}{c|c|r}
\text{source}&\text{target}&\text{multiplicity}\\\hline
(0,1)&(1,1)&1\\
(1,1)&(2,1)&h\\
(1,2)&(2,2)&h\\
(2,2)&(3,2)&1.
\end{array}
\end{equation}
The vertical arrows are their reflections in $p=q$.

\item Under the cited decomposition, an arrow contributes its target
to Bott--Chern cohomology and its source to Aeppli cohomology;
a dot contributes to both and a square to neither. The induced
isomorphisms in Lemma~8 respect Definition~\ref{I:def:BC-Aeppli}.
Counting the displayed arrows and their reflections gives every
entry of \eqref{inv:eq:C-BC-A}. Kernels, images and these quotients
commute with direct sums, including the possible infinite sum of
squares.

\end{enumerate}
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[A projective-space component in a point-star refinement]\hypertarget{tag-02ZL}{}\label{push:src:A.2.3}\label{push:A23}
\StatementRecord{push:src:A.2.3}{proved}{02ZL}{\SeriesRef{push:src:A.2.1}, \SeriesRef{push:src:A.2.4}, \SeriesRef{push:src:A.2.5}, \SeriesRef{fam:src:E.1.1}}
The first maximal-cone star of Proposition~\ref{push:src:A.2.1} contributes a central normalization component $\mathbb P^3$. There is no proper bimeromorphic holomorphic map from this component to $R$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The star is the ordinary blow-up of a point in a smooth four-dimensional chart, with exceptional divisor $\mathbb P^3$. The projective-bundle basis gives $b_2(R)=b_2(S)+1=11$, while $b_2(\mathbb P^3)=1$. A proper bimeromorphic map $f:\mathbb P^3\to R$ has topological degree one, and the projection formula gives
\[
 \int_{\mathbb P^3}f^*a\,f^*b=\int_Rab
 \qquad(a\in H^2(R;\mathbb Q),\ b\in H^4(R;\mathbb Q)).
\]
Poincar\'e duality would make $f^*$ injective in degree two, contradicting these ranks.
\end{proof}

