The Cartier-divisor construction used in Proposition~\ref{push:src:B.4.7} is the construction of \citet[construction, p.~85; Definition~1.1, p.~86]{Fujimoto1990AB}. His Remark~1.4 attributes non-K\"ahler torus degenerations to Ueno. His Proposition~1.14, pp.~92--93, extends principal elliptic bundles across subsets of codimension at least three; Example~1.15, pp.~93--94, gives a principal-bundle extension failure in codimension two and leaves general relative compactification open in that example. Hopf components occur in the semistable degenerations of \citet[Theorem~1.4, preprint p.~7, and Example~2.1, p.~13]{Nishiguchi1988IIRoot}. The diagonal Hopf quotients, elliptic-conductor identifications and normal lines in \citet[Propositions~3.1--3.4, preprint pp.~6--9]{SchroeerSiebert2002Root} provide related explicit models. Proposition~\ref{push:src:B.4.7} proves its own proper family using the stated Cartier-divisor cocycle.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[Topology determined by the fan data]\hypertarget{tag-02ZV}{}\label{push:src:A.4.5}\label{push:A45}
\StatementRecord{push:src:A.4.5}{proved}{02ZV}{\SeriesRef{push:src:A.2.4}, \SeriesRef{push:src:A.4.4}, \SeriesRef{push:src:A.4.7}, \SeriesRef{push:src:A.4.8}, \SeriesRef{push:src:A.4.9}}
For an output of Theorem~\ref{push:src:A.2.4},
Theorem~\ref{push:src:A.4.7} computes the Euler number, fundamental
group and nearby Euler function, and Theorem~\ref{push:src:A.4.8}
gives all integral homology groups and the marked monodromy.
Proposition~\ref{push:src:A.4.9} identifies the integral maps in the
normalization calculation through its direct-image splitting and
unit exceptional-fibre diagonals. All these calculations use the
same marked cone data.
\end{remark}

